E = [
("01-01","The New Year's Day that gave U2 its first anthem","Rock","1983","Bono wrote the lyric imagining Poland's Solidarity movement, but the song started as a bassline Adam Clayton stumbled into while trying to play Visage's 'Fade to Grey'. The Edge's icy piano was a happy accident from a keyboard left in the room.",["The band nearly cut the track entirely, convinced it was going nowhere","Bono has said the words came out 'about a couple separated by war' before he knew why"],"guitar"),
("01-02","How a broken speaker invented the fuzz guitar","Rock","1961","On Marty Robbins' 'Don't Worry', a faulty channel in the studio's mixing console distorted Grady Martin's bass solo into a snarl nobody had heard before. Engineers tried to fix it; the producer kept it.",["Nashville session players began requesting 'the broken sound' by name","It led directly to the first commercial fuzz pedal two years later"],"mixer"),
("01-03","The drum break that built hip-hop","Funk","1969","The Winstons cut 'Amen, Brother' as a B-side throwaway. Its six-second drum break, played by Gregory Coleman in a single take, became the most sampled passage in recorded music.",["The break powered entire genres: jungle, drum and bass, and countless hip-hop records","The band saw no royalties from the sampling boom for decades"],"drums"),
("01-04","Why Motown mixed its records on a car speaker","Soul","1964","Berry Gordy kept a cheap car-dashboard speaker wired into the Hitsville control room. If a mix didn't hit on that tinny cone, it went back, because that's where America actually heard music.",["Engineers called the test 'the Detroit ear'","Motown also boosted mid-range so songs cut through AM radio static"],"radio"),
("01-05","The bassline Paul McCartney built from a mistake","Rock","1966","On 'Paperback Writer', McCartney wanted a bass so loud it would rattle radios. Engineer Ken Townsend rigged a loudspeaker as a microphone to capture the low end, a trick no one had tried.",["EMI's rulebook technically forbade the setup","The result was the loudest bass ever pressed to a Beatles single to that point"],"guitar"),
("01-06","Kraftwerk built their own instruments because none existed","Electronic","1974","For 'Autobahn', the band constructed custom electronic drums and vocoders in their Kling Klang studio. They were chasing the sound of a car trip, not a band.",["The whooshing traffic sounds are synthesized, not recorded","The 22-minute original was cut to three minutes for radio and became a global hit"],"synth"),
("01-07","The piano on 'Layla' was never meant to be there","Rock","1970","Drummer Jim Gordon was caught playing a private piano melody during downtime. Eric Clapton heard it through the studio glass and insisted it become the song's second half.",["The coda was recorded months after the main track","The two halves were spliced together with tape, not re-performed"],"piano"),
("01-08","Elvis recorded his first single for his mother","Rock","1953","At 18, Presley paid Sun Studio's $4 fee to cut 'My Happiness' as a gift. Receptionist Marion Keisker secretly taped a copy because she liked his voice.",["Her note read: 'Good ballad singer. Hold.'","That saved tape led Sam Phillips to call him back a year later"],"mic"),
("01-09","Joni Mitchell tuned her guitar wrong on purpose","Folk","1971","Polio left Mitchell's left hand weak, so she invented dozens of open tunings that let her voice chords with fewer fingers. Those tunings gave 'Blue' its floating, unplaceable harmony.",["She used more than 50 distinct tunings across her career","Guitarists still struggle to cover her songs in standard tuning"],"guitar"),
("01-10","The studio trick that made 'Strawberry Fields' impossible","Rock","1966","Lennon liked two different takes recorded in different keys at different speeds. George Martin sped one up and slowed the other until they met in the middle.",["The splice happens exactly one minute in","Martin later called the odds of it working 'astronomical'"],"tape"),
("01-11","Aretha rewrote 'Respect' and took it from Otis","Soul","1967","Otis Redding's original was a man asking for respect at home. Aretha Franklin flipped the perspective, added the spelled-out hook and the 'sock it to me' backing from her sisters.",["Redding reportedly said 'that girl has stolen my song'","The R-E-S-P-E-C-T spelling was invented in the studio that day"],"mic"),
("01-12","Why disco records used real strings and a metronome","Disco","1975","Philadelphia producers paired full orchestras with a rigid four-on-the-floor kick so DJs could beat-match by hand. The click track was the secret that made mixing possible.",["Engineers printed the tempo on the record label for DJs","This is why so many disco 12-inches sit near 120 beats per minute"],"strings"),
("01-13","Brian Eno wrote rules on cards to break writer's block","Art Rock","1975","Frustrated in the studio, Eno printed 'Oblique Strategies' — cards with instructions like 'Honour thy error as a hidden intention'. Musicians drew one when stuck.",["Bowie used the deck heavily during the Berlin albums","One card simply reads 'Do nothing for as long as possible'"],"synth"),
("01-14","The Roland TR-808 flopped before it changed everything","Hip-Hop","1980","Roland discontinued the drum machine after three years because it sounded fake next to real drums. Producers bought them cheap secondhand, and its booming kick became the spine of hip-hop.",["The 808's sound came from faulty transistors Roland bought as a batch","When that supply ran out, the machine could not be made again"],"drums"),
("01-15","Stevie Wonder played nearly every instrument on 'Innervisions'","Soul","1973","Wonder recorded drums, keys, bass and harmonica himself, layering parts alone in the studio through the night. Engineers worked in shifts to keep up with him.",["He often worked 48-hour sessions without leaving","The clavinet sound on 'Higher Ground' was cut in about three hours"],"piano"),
("01-16","Dolly Parton wrote two standards on the same day","Country","1973","Parton has said she wrote 'Jolene' and 'I Will Always Love You' in a single writing session. One was about a bank teller flirting with her husband; the other about leaving her mentor.",["Elvis wanted to record 'I Will Always Love You' but his manager demanded publishing","She said no, and kept the rights that later made her a fortune"],"guitar"),
("01-17","The Wall of Sound was built by crowding the room","Pop","1963","Phil Spector packed multiple pianos, guitars and percussionists into a small echo-heavy studio and recorded them as one blurred mass. The point was density, not clarity.",["Musicians played the same part simultaneously to thicken it","The reverb came from a real echo chamber, a concrete room with a speaker"],"mixer"),
("01-18","Bob Marley's band recorded in a room with no separation","Reggae","1973","The Wailers cut early sides live in cramped Kingston studios where every mic bled into the others. That bleed is why the records feel like one breathing organism.",["Engineers used the leakage as natural reverb","Bass was cut deliberately loud for Jamaican sound-system playback"],"mixer"),
("01-19","Miles Davis handed out no sheet music for 'Kind of Blue'","Jazz","1959","Davis gave his players only modal sketches minutes before recording. Most of the album is first takes by musicians hearing the material for the first time.",["Bill Evans wrote the liner notes comparing it to Japanese brush painting","It remains the best-selling jazz album ever made"],"horn"),
("01-20","Prince played 27 instruments on his debut","Funk","1978","Warner Bros. doubted a 19-year-old could produce himself, so Prince recorded a demo playing every part. He got full creative control, almost unheard of for a new artist.",["He reportedly recorded the album in about five weeks","His contract made him one of the youngest self-producing artists on a major label"],"guitar"),
("01-21","The Fairlight sampler cost as much as a house","Pop","1979","The first commercial digital sampler sold for tens of thousands of dollars, limiting it to stars like Kate Bush and Peter Gabriel. It let musicians play any recorded sound from a keyboard.",["Bush used it to turn breaking glass into an instrument","Its low memory forced the short, clipped samples that define the era"],"sampler"),
("01-22","Why 'Bohemian Rhapsody' took three weeks of overdubs","Rock","1975","Queen layered their vocal harmonies up to 180 times, bouncing tape so often it wore thin. Held to the light, the master was nearly transparent.",["The band sang 10-12 hours a day for the opera section","No synthesizers were used; the album sleeve said so proudly"],"mixer"),
("01-23","Nina Simone trained as a classical pianist, not a singer","Jazz","1957","Denied a place at the Curtis Institute, Simone took a bar job that required singing. She had only ever intended to play Bach, and that classical phrasing never left her.",["Her improvisations often quote Bach fugues mid-song","She sang publicly for the first time only because the club owner insisted"],"piano"),
("01-24","The Clash recorded 'London Calling' with a producer who threw chairs","Punk","1979","Guy Stevens motivated takes by swinging ladders and pouring wine on the piano. The chaos was intentional; he wanted performances, not perfection.",["Engineer Bill Price quietly kept the tape rolling through the mayhem","The title track's bass was played by Paul Simonon, who learned bass in the band"],"guitar"),
("01-25","Hip-hop's first hit used a disco band playing live","Hip-Hop","1979","'Rapper's Delight' didn't sample Chic's 'Good Times' — session musicians replayed the groove because sampling technology barely existed.",["Chic's Nile Rodgers sued and won a songwriting credit","The record ran nearly 15 minutes on the 12-inch"],"turntable"),
("01-26","Sly Stone recorded an album on an early drum machine","Funk","1971","For 'There's a Riot Goin' On', Stone used a primitive rhythm box instead of a drummer, then layered tape until the sound smeared. Critics called it murky; he meant it.",["He recorded much of it alone in a home studio","The tape was overdubbed so many times the hiss became part of the sound"],"drums"),
("01-27","Mozart's 'Requiem' was finished by someone else","Classical","1791","Mozart died leaving the work incomplete. His student Franz Xaver Süssmayr finished it, and his widow hid the fact to secure the commission payment.",["Scholars still argue over which bars are actually Mozart's","The anonymous patron wanted to pass it off as his own composition"],"strings"),
("01-28","Robert Johnson recorded facing the corner of a room","Blues","1936","In a San Antonio hotel room, Johnson played into the corner. For years it was called superstition; engineers now note it created natural bass reinforcement.",["Only 29 songs of his survive","The corner technique is a real acoustic trick still used today"],"guitar"),
("01-29","Talking Heads built 'Remain in Light' from loops before loops existed","New Wave","1980","The band recorded single-bar grooves and spliced them into endless cycles by hand with razor blades and tape, inspired by Fela Kuti's Afrobeat.",["Each member recorded parts separately, never playing the song together","Eno and Byrne assembled the arrangements afterward"],"tape"),
("01-30","The Beatles' last live show was on a roof","Rock","1969","With no venue and no plan, the band played atop their Savile Row office. London police shut it down after 42 minutes, which is why the film has an ending.",["Office workers watched from surrounding rooftops","It was the final time all four performed publicly together"],"crowd"),
("01-31","Why reggae's bass sits so far forward","Reggae","1968","Jamaican records were made for outdoor sound systems where bass carried across a street. Engineers cut low frequencies hot and pulled the treble back.",["Producers tested mixes on actual street speakers before release","This is the root of dub, born from stripping vocals off those mixes"],"radio"),
]
